Run the mutants and report what the tests noticed. Every accepted mutant unless one is named

Usage: rust-mutants run [OPTIONS] [-- <ARGS>...]

Arguments:
  [ARGS]...
          Arguments for the test harness itself

Options:
      --color <WHEN>
          Whether to paint the output. `auto` paints a terminal that has not set `NO_COLOR`

          Possible values:
          - auto:   Colour a terminal that has not asked to go without
          - always: Always
          - never:  Never
          
          [default: auto]

      --root <DIR>
          The workspace root. Defaults to the working directory

      --config <FILE>
          Read this configuration file instead of the one in the workspace root

      --no-config
          Read no configuration file at all

      --tier <TIER>
          Which tier of operators to apply

          Possible values:
          - balanced: The operators worth running everywhere
          - strong:   Balanced, plus the bitwise and compound-assignment families
          - all:      Every operator, statement deletion included

      --operator <RULE>
          Apply exactly these operators, by name. Repeatable

      --include <GLOB>
          Only mutate files matching this pattern. Repeatable

      --exclude <GLOB>
          Mutate nothing in the files matching this pattern; they are still compiled. The other half
          of `--include`. Repeatable

      --omit <GLOB>
          Leave files matching this pattern out of the copy the run works in. A file a crate
          declares as a module leaves a tree that does not compile. Repeatable

      --changed
          Mutate only the files that differ from `HEAD`, committed and not. Narrows `--include`
          rather than widening it

      --changed-from <REV>
          Mutate only the files that differ from this revision

  -p, --package <NAME>
          Only mutate these packages. Repeatable

      --allow-outside <DIR>
          Let the build read this directory from outside the root, repeatably, copying it where the
          tree reaches it

      --features <LIST>
          Compile with these cargo features. Repeatable, and each may be a comma-separated list

      --build-target <TRIPLE>
          Compile for this target triple instead of the host. Not `run --target`, which names a test
          target

      --profile <NAME>
          Compile with this cargo profile

      --build-jobs <N>
          How many compilation jobs cargo may run at once

  -j, --jobs <N|auto|all>
          How many mutants to measure at once: a count, `auto` for as many as the machine has capped
          at four, or `all` for every one

      --skip-target <PKG/KIND/NAME>
          Never start this target, by the id a report names it with. Repeatable

      --timeout <DURATION>
          How long one mutant execution may take before it is retried serially, as in `90s` or `5m`

      --trace[=<DIR>]
          Record what the run does, as JSON Lines. Written with `--trace=DIR`; bare `--trace` writes
          beside the report, and every other command under `<reports>/traces/`

      --offline
          Never touch the network

      --locked
          Refuse to change `Cargo.lock`

      --keep-temp
          Keep the snapshot and the target directory instead of removing them

      --no-verify
          Skip the run of every target with nothing active

      --coverage
          Measure once which target reached what, and run a mutant only against the targets that
          reached it

      --no-coverage
          Run every mutant against every target, measuring no coverage and proving nothing about
          reach

      --no-touch
          Do not ask the guards which of each target's tests reached them, and so run every test of
          every target that could

      --equivalence
          After the run, ask the compiler whether each survivor's mutation is one it renders at all

      --no-doctests
          Leave a library's documented examples out of the targets

      --all-features
          Compile with every feature of every selected package

      --no-default-features
          Compile with the default features off

      --mutant <PREFIX>
          Run only this mutant, by identity or by any prefix that names exactly one

      --target <TARGET>
          With `--mutant`, run only this target, by `package/kind/name` or by name

      --test <TEST>
          With `--mutant`, run only this test, by its libtest path

      --shard <K/N>
          Run only this part of the catalog, as `K/N`. Every part of one tree cuts the same way, so
          the parts together are the whole

      --no-report
          Do not write a run report under the report directory

      --no-cache
          Remeasure coverage, the passing baseline, and every mutant instead of reading back what an
          earlier run of this exact tree established

      --ui <MODE>
          How much the run says while it is happening

          Possible values:
          - auto:  A terminal gets the overwriting tally, anything else gets the plain lines
          - plain: One line per phase and per mutant, and a tally every ten and at the end
          - quiet: Nothing until the summary
          
          [default: auto]

      --json
          Write the run as it happens, one JSON object per line, for a program rather than a person

      --fail-fast
          Stop at the first thing a reader has to act on rather than measuring the rest

      --rule <NAME>
          Measure only mutants of this rule. Repeatable

      --family <NAME>
          Measure only mutants of this family. Repeatable

      --skip-rule <NAME>
          Never measure mutants of this rule. Repeatable

      --skip-family <NAME>
          Never measure mutants of this family. Repeatable

      --file <PATH>
          Measure only mutants in this file, and optionally only these lines, as `PATH[:FROM[-TO]]`.
          Repeatable

      --id <PREFIX>
          Measure only mutants whose identity starts with this. Repeatable

      --from-report [<RUN>]
          Measure only the mutants a stored run left with this outcome. The newest run when no
          directory is named

      --outcome <OUTCOME>
          With `--from-report`, the outcome to take from it
          
          [default: survived]

      --dry-run
          Prepare and verify, then say what a run would cost, without executing a mutant

      --run-id <NAME>
          Name this run, which is what its report directory is called. Letters, digits, `.`, `_` and
          `-`

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
